      "id": "S",
      "title": "Job selection: priorities, tenant rotation, dependencies, rate limits",
      "kind": "implement",
      "goal": "Implement everything that decides which job run_once picks, in jobrunner/runner.py and jobrunner/store.py. These three features all change the same selection in run_once, so they are one sub-task. (1) Persistence: store the new Job fields (priority, tenant, depends_on, definition_id, cancel_reason) in the jobs table (JobStore.add takes them as keyword arguments, including next_run_at for run_at and definition_id) and make submit store them. (2) Replace the in-memory heap in run_once with a selection over the stored PENDING jobs as SPEC.md 'Running jobs' says: a job can run when it is due, every dependency SUCCEEDED, and its kind is under its rate limit; among those pick the highest priority, then the tenant whose most recent start is oldest (a tenant that never started first; persist the starts so this survives a restart), then next_run_at, then id. (3) Record every start (kind, tenant, time) in SQLite for rate limits and tenant turns. (4) Dependencies (SPEC.md 'Dependencies'): submit checks depends_on; when a job becomes FAILED or CANCELLED in any code path (run_once, cancel, restart recovery) cancel its PENDING dependents downstream with cancel_reason 'dependency_failed'; a job submitted with an already failed or cancelled dependency is cancelled at once; cancel() sets cancel_reason 'cancel_requested'. (5) Implement set_priority, queue, tenants, dependents, set_rate_limit, clear_rate_limit and list_rate_limits. Write tests/test_selection.py.",
